**[00:03:35] Person D:** That's an important constraint to weigh. It doesn't have to be all-or-nothing on day one — some organizations start with more guided tooling and add flexibility as the team's skills grow. What matters most to you in terms of what the platform needs to be good at first?

**[00:04:05] Person B:** Speed to first real report. If it takes us six months to get anything usable out of a new platform, the business is going to lose confidence before we've proven anything.

**[00:04:20] Person C:** And from my side — manageability. My infrastructure team is small. Whatever we pick, we can't be standing up and patching a lot of custom infrastructure ourselves. I'd rather lean into managed services even if it costs a bit more, than take on more operational burden.

**[00:04:50] Person E:** That's useful — leaning toward managed services is consistent with either Fabric or Databricks on Azure, since both are largely PaaS. Let's talk storage. Regardless of which compute engine we land on, we'd recommend landing data in Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 as the common foundation — that keeps you flexible even if the compute layer decision evolves later.

**[00:05:30] Person A:** That makes sense to me — don't want to be locked into one engine at the storage layer.

**[00:05:40] Person D:** Let's talk ingestion. Your source is still that transactional database with the thousand tables. What's the appetite for real-time or near-real-time data versus sticking with batch for now?

**[00:06:05] Person B:** For most of what we report on, nightly is genuinely fine — nobody's asking for real-time revenue numbers. The one exception is Supply Chain — they've mentioned wanting to see inventory positions more frequently, closer to hourly, because stockouts are a real cost to them.

