**[00:08:50] Person E:** It's a fair concern with any cloud-native platform — compute costs scale with usage, which is the whole point of the elasticity, but it does mean forecasting looks different from a fixed hardware budget. We'd recommend starting with a capped dev/test environment and close cost monitoring during Milestone 1, so Finance has real data points before we scale usage across all four business domains.

**[00:09:20] Person A:** That's reasonable. I'd want that cost tracking visible to Finance from week one, not after the fact.

**[00:09:35] Person D:** Noted — we'll capture that as a delivery requirement, not just a nice-to-have.

**[00:09:45] Person B:** Can I raise something about the marts? Whatever we land on, I really want the new platform to fix the duplicated-logic problem we talked about last time — Finance and Sales defining revenue differently. Is that something the platform choice affects?

**[00:10:15] Person D:** It does, actually — this is a strong argument for building a shared semantic layer once, rather than replicating business logic per mart again. Both Fabric and Databricks support that pattern; Fabric's approach is more tied into Power BI's own semantic models, Databricks would pair with a separate metrics layer tool. Given your business already lives in Power BI, that's a point in Fabric's favor.

**[00:10:50] Person A:** That's a meaningful point for me. I don't want us to rebuild the same duplication problem on new infrastructure.

**[00:11:05] Person C:** Agreed. Whatever we pick, I want that written down as a hard requirement, not just an aspiration.

